# How do we get from one person to a demand curve?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Adding up many buyers Why market demand is smoother than any one person's"
next: {"title":"Why might you work less when your wage rises?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/79-why-might-you-work-less-when-your-wage.cast"}

## Adding sideways
domain: {"x":[0,7.5],"y":[0,10]}
vars:
    P:
      value: 5
      min: 0.5
      max: 9.8
    qA:
      expr: min(3, max(0, floor((9 - P)/3) + 1))
    qE:
      expr: min(3, max(0, floor((7.5 - P)/3) + 1))
    Qm:
      expr: qA + qE
sources:
    - id: cohen2016
      title: 'Using Big Data to Estimate Consumer Surplus: The Case of Uber'
      authors: Peter Cohen, Robert Hahn, Jonathan Hall, Steven Levitt, Robert Metcalfe
      year: 2016
      finding: Nearly 50 million UberX sessions in Chicago, Los Angeles, New York and San Francisco (2015), each a decision to request a ride at the price shown or not, used to estimate demand elasticities along the demand curve (NBER Working Paper 22627)
      url: https://www.nber.org/papers/w22627

    text big "≈ 50 million" at.percent [50,82] cites ["cohen2016"] font_size 64 hidden true
    icon phone at.percent [30,45] size 110 set twemoji icon_look picture of "mobile phone" hidden true
    text yesno "yes or no?" below phone gap 20 #b5482e font_size 36 hidden true
    text where "Uber · 4 US cities · 2015" #8f887c at.percent [50,16] cites ["cohen2016"] font_size 26 hidden true
    path sketch smooth thick #3d3833 points [[600,440],[660,360],[760,305],[880,280]] hidden true
    text l_sketch "smooth demand" below sketch gap 14 font_size 26 hidden true
    axes ax x_label "Visits a week" y_label "Price (P)" cross corner ticks.x [1,2,3,4,5,6] ticks.y true hidden true
    curve ada thick #b5482e expr "9 - 3*(max(1, ceil(x)) - 1) + 0.0001*sin(300*x)" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    label l_ada "Ada" right #b5482e attach_to ada hidden true
    curve erik thick #2f6b8f expr "7.5 - 3*(max(1, ceil(x)) - 1) + 0.0001*sin(300*x)" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    label l_erik "Erik" right #2f6b8f attach_to erik hidden true
    text vsum "$9 + $7.50 = $16.50?" #8f887c at.data [4.6,8.6] font_size 30 hidden true
    mark strike around vsum kind strike hidden true
    path pline dashed #8f887c points [[0,5],[6.6,5]] data true bind {"points.0.1":"P","points.1.1":"P"} hidden true
    text l_p "P = ${P:2}" #3d3833 at.data [6.1,5.5] font_size 26 bind {"at.data.1":"P + 0.5"} hidden true
    path segA #b5482e points [[0,5],[2,5]] data true bind {"points.0.1":"P","points.1.0":"qA","points.1.1":"P"} style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    path segE #2f6b8f points [[2,5],[3,5]] data true bind {"points.0.0":"qA","points.0.1":"P","points.1.0":"qA + qE","points.1.1":"P"} style.stroke_width 7 hidden true
    dot dot #3d3833 at.x 3 at.y 5 bind {"at.x":"Qm","at.y":"P"} hidden true
    label readout "{qA} + {qE} = {Qm}" above-right #3d3833 attach_to dot hidden true
    text l_market "Market" at.data [6.4,2.4] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve market thick #3d3833 expr "9 - 1.5*(max(1, ceil(x)) - 1) + 0.0001*sin(300*x)" x_from 0 x_to 6 hidden true

    card title "Adding sideways"

How do we get from one person's choices to a demand curve for a whole market?
    draw phone

B: Here's a real case. Economists studied (@draw big@) nearly fifty million sessions on the Uber app in 2015, a (@draw where@)cross four big American cities.

Each session en (@draw yesno@)ds in a yes or a no: request the ride at the price shown, or don't. Yet from all those cl (@draw sketch l_sketch@)icks they estimated one smooth demand curve.

    erase big phone yesno where sketch l_sketch

How do lumpy yeses and noes add up to a smooth line?
    draw ax

    pause 0.4

Start small: two people and the sauna on Harbourtown's quay. The prices are illustrative.
    highlight ax

Ada would pa (@draw ada@)y nine dollars for her first visit a week, six for a second, three for a third. Her dema (@draw l_ada@)nd is a staircase.

B: Erik values his visits at seven fifty, four fifty and a dollar fifty. So the first visit is worth nine p (@draw vsum@)lus seven fifty to the market: sixteen fifty?
    draw erik l_erik

Tempting, but nobody pays sixteen fifty for one visit. Fi (@draw pline l_p@)x the price instead: say five dollars.
    draw strike

    erase vsum strike

    quiz "At $5 a visit, how many visits a week do Ada and Erik buy together?"
        right: Three: Ada's two steps at nine and six dollars, plus Erik's one at seven fifty.
        wrong: Count the steps of each staircase at or above the five-dollar line, then add: here, three.
        * 2
        + 3
        * 5

Ada has (@draw segA@) two steps above the l (@draw segE@)ine, Erik one: three  (@draw dot readout@)visits. At a given price we add quantities, sideways. Economists (@highlight segA segE@) call it adding horizontally.

    animate P 9.6 duration 1

Now slide the price down and add again at every price. The dot traces the market's staircase: six smaller steps instead of three.
    animate P 0.8 duration 7 easing linear trail.of dot

B: Two peop (@draw market l_market@)le, and the steps are already half as tall. Now picture the whole town.
    erase segA segE pline l_p readout dot dot_trail

## Why the stairs melt
domain: {"x":[0,3.8],"y":[0,10]}
vars:
    'N':
      value: 1
      min: 1
      max: 1000
      step: 1
      decimals: 0
    spread:
      value: 1
      min: 0
      max: 1
      step: 0.01
      decimals: 2
    pct:
      expr: 100*spread

    axes ax x_label "Visits per person" y_label "Price (P)" cross corner ticks.x [1,2,3] ticks.y true hidden true
    curve dem thick #3d3833 expr "9 - 3*(ceil(max(1, ceil(N*x))/N) - 1) - 3*spread*(max(1, ceil(N*x)) - (ceil(max(1, ceil(N*x))/N) - 1)*N - 1)/N + 0.0001*sin(300*x)" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    text l_dem "Demand per person" at.data [2.45,3.9] font_size 28 hidden true
    text r_n "buyers: {N:0}" #2f6b8f at.data [0.75,2.7] font_size 40 hidden true
    text r_s "differences: {pct:0}%" #b5482e at.data [0.75,1.5] font_size 30 hidden true
    scratch mathcard top-right work [{"tex":"q_1 = 10 - P,\\quad q_2 = 6 - P"},{"tex":"Q(P) = q_1(P) + q_2(P)"},{"tex":"P > 6:\\; Q = 10 - P"},{"tex":"P < 6:\\; Q = 16 - 2P"}] font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Why the stairs melt"

B: Adding sideways turned two staircases into one with finer steps. Let's add a whole town.
    draw ax

B: To keep the pi (@draw dem@)cture the same size, I'll divide by the number of buyers: visits a week (@draw r_n@) per person. With one buyer, it's Ada's three big steps.

So what does a second buyer do?
    highlight dem

B: His steps, like  (@animate N 2 duration 1.2@)Erik's, fall in between Ada's, so the steps halv (@animate N 10 duration 1.2@)e. With ten buyers they're a tenth as tall.

B: With a thousand, each step is three tenths of a cent tall. Your eye se (@draw l_dem@)es a smooth line.
    animate N 1000 duration 3

So it's sheer numbers that smooth the curve?
    highlight r_n

B: Not quite. Keep the thousand buyers, but m (@animate spread 0 duration 2 ghost true@)ake every one a copy of Ada.
    draw r_s

Ada's same three big steps, a thousand times over. So the smoothness comes from (@animate spread 1 duration 2@) buyers being different, each with their own limits.
    highlight dem

    erase dem_ghost

Try it: push the number of buyers up with the differences at zero, then turn the differences up and watch the stairs melt.
    explore params ["N","spread"]

    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"after"}] then after

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own

@math

    erase mathq

Write each buyer's demand as a quantity at a price: say one buyer's is ten minus P, another's six minus P.
    draw mathcard_box mathcard_line_1

Market demand adds them at the same price.
    draw mathcard_line_2

Above six dollars only the first buys, so Q is ten minus P.
    draw mathcard_line_3

Below six both buy, so Q is sixteen minus two P: the curve has a kink where the second buyer joins.
    draw mathcard_line_4

    erase mathcard

@after

    erase mathq

    erase r_n r_s

    animate N 10 spread 1 duration 1

    erase l_dem

B: The kinks never go away. A market demand curve is a staircase whose steps are too small to see.
    camera center.canvas [380,460] zoom 2.2

    camera reset true

    draw l_dem

## When buyers watch each other
domain: {"x":[0,3.8],"y":[0,10]}
vars: {"k":{"value":3,"min":1.5,"max":6}}
sources:
    - id: leibenstein1950
      title: Bandwagon, Snob, and Veblen Effects in the Theory of Consumers' Demand
      authors: Harvey Leibenstein
      year: 1950
      finding: 'When one person''s demand depends on how many others buy (bandwagon, snob and Veblen effects), market demand is not the simple sum of individual demand curves; bandwagon effects make it more elastic, snob effects less (Quarterly Journal of Economics 64(2): 183–207)'
    - id: becker1962
      title: Irrational Behavior and Economic Theory
      authors: Gary S. Becker
      year: 1962
      finding: 'Even households choosing at random within their budgets produce downward-sloping market demand curves, because a price rise shrinks the set of affordable bundles (Journal of Political Economy 70(1): 1–13)'

    axes ax x_label "Visits per person" y_label "Price (P)" cross corner ticks.x [1,2,3] ticks.y true hidden true
    curve plain thick #3d3833 expr "9 - 3*x" x_from 0 x_to 3 hidden true
    text l_plain "Plain sum" at.data [2.3,3.6] font_size 28 hidden true
    curve crowd thick #8a5fa8 expr "4.5 - k*(x - 1.5)" x_from 0 x_to 3 bind.x_from "max(0, 1.5 - 4.5/k)" bind.x_to "min(3.7, 1.5 + 4.5/k)" hidden true
    text l_band "Bandwagon" #8a5fa8 at.data [3.1,2.3] cites ["leibenstein1950"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text l_snob "Snob" #8a5fa8 at.data [0.95,8.6] cites ["leibenstein1950"] font_size 30 hidden true
    text st_1 "Downward slope: consensus" at.data [2.55,9] cites ["becker1962"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text st_2 "Crowd effects: contested" #b5482e at.data [2.55,8] cites ["leibenstein1950"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text leib "Leibenstein, 1950" #8a5fa8 at.data [2.75,8.6] cites ["leibenstein1950"] font_size 32 hidden true
    text echo "≈ 50 million clicks" at.data [2.6,8.4] font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "When buyers watch each other"

So the market curve is a sum of staircases. But adding sideways makes  (@draw l_plain@)one quiet assumption: each buyer decides alone.
    draw ax plain

B: A sauna isn't like that. I go when my friends go.
    highlight plain

Harvey Leibenstein called that a bandwagon effect, back in 1950: wanting something more because others buy it.
    draw leib

    fade plain l_plain to 0.3

B: Then a price cut pulls in a few buyers, and they pull in their friends.
    draw crowd

    erase leib

So market demand comes out flatter than the plain sum: quan (@draw l_band@)tity reacts more to price.
    animate k 1.8 duration 2

    erase l_band

B: And snobs work the other way (@animate k 5 duration 2.5@). They drop out as the crowd arrives, so demand (@draw l_snob@) comes out steeper.

    erase l_snob

Either way, market demand is no longer a simple sum of se (@erase crowd@)parate people.
    animate k 3 duration 1.5

    fade plain l_plain to 1

B: So how sure are we of the basic picture?
    highlight plain

The downward s (@draw st_1@)lope is sturdy. Gary Becker showed in 1962 that even buyers who chose at ran (@highlight plain@)dom would trace one, because their budgets bind.

B: How big the crowd effect (@draw st_2@)s are is another matter: large for messaging apps, tiny for salt, and argued market by market.

    quiz "Ten thousand identical people each buy one winter coat if it costs under $100. What does market demand look like?"
        intro: Three questions on new cases.
        right: A cliff at $100: identical buyers all jump at the same price, so ten thousand of them make one giant step.
        wrong: What made the steps small: the number of buyers, or how different they are? Here, a cliff at $100.
        * A smooth downward line
        + A cliff at $100
        * A vertical line at 10,000 coats

    quiz "A messaging app is worth more to you the more of your friends use it. Its price falls. Compared with adding up separate buyers, how many new users come?" voice b
        right: More: each new user makes the app worth more to their friends, who join too. A bandwagon flattens demand.
        wrong: Each new user makes the app worth more to the others: here, more.
        + More
        * The same
        * Fewer

    quiz "Ada and Erik would build one sauna and share it, both using it at once. To find what it is worth to the pair, what do we add?"
        right: Their prices: one sauna serves both, so it is worth what Ada would pay plus what Erik would pay. Sideways adding is for things each buys separately.
        wrong: One sauna serves both at once: here, their prices for the one sauna.
        * Their quantities at each price
        + Their prices for the one sauna

So fifty million yes (@draw echo@)-or-no clicks drew one smooth curve because th (@highlight plain@)ere were millions of riders, and no two had quite the same limit.
    erase st_1 st_2

